 ## Overview
-Hierarchical Sampling (Inverse CDF Sampling) generates importance samples along a ray in volume rendering by inverting the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the rendering contribution [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of finding the ray parameter $t$ corresponding to a target cumulative density value $y$, allowing samples to be drawn directly from transmittance estimates rather than computing expensive integrals [S2, unofficial]. Unlike standard NeRF hierarchical sampling, which interpolates exponentiated densities (probabilities) and requires auxiliary losses, this method interpolates the density field directly (piecewise linear or constant), offering better numerical stability for end-to-end optimization [S1, unofficial]. It enables Monte Carlo estimates with fewer ray points and allows gradients to back-propagate through the sampling process to the density field parameters [S1, unofficial].
+Hierarchical Sampling (Inverse CDF Sampling) generates fine-grained depth samples along a ray by sampling from a probability density function (PDF) derived from coarse rendering weights [S3, S4, S5, unofficial]. It uses inverse transform sampling on the cumulative distribution function (CDF) to concentrate samples in high-density regions, improving density estimation and reducing variance compared to uniform sampling [S2, S8, unofficial]. It builds on [Sampling and Jittering](2516-sampling-and-jittering.md) by using importance sampling to refine coarse passes [G].
 
